- This dataset is based on Human Resources (HR) data from a company (IBM), focused on understanding why employees leave their jobs, also called employee Attrition.
- The dataset helps you predict whether an employee will leave the company (Attrition = Yes) or stay (Attrition = No) based on their job satisfaction, salary, work-life balance, overtime, and other factors.

Total Employees: 1,470.
Gender Breakdown: 60% Female (882) vs 40% Male (588).
Department Distribution: R&D dominates at 65.37%, Sales: 30.34%, HR: Very small portion at 4.29%.
Marital Status:, Married: 31.97%, Single: 45.78% (the largest group), Divorced: 22.24%. 
Single employees may form a higher-risk group for attrition if not engaged properly.

Work Behavior & Satisfaction.
Average Job Involvement: 2.7 (on a 1–4 scale)
Job Satisfaction: 2.7
Work-Life Balance: 2.8
Relationship Satisfaction: 2.7
These are below ideal — hovering around average rather than high, which could signal low engagement or potential dissatisfaction.

Experience & Tenure.
Average Age: 36.9
Total Working Years: 11.3
Years at Company: 7
Years in Current Role: 4.23
Job Level: 2.1
Workforce is mid-career and stable, but there may be limited upward movement, as shown by: Low average job level (2.1), Long duration in current roles (4.23 years).
Compensation & Benefits: Average Salary Hike %: 15.2%, Average Salary Hike Amount: 849.3$, Stock Option Level: 0.8 (low).
Stock options are rarely used as an incentive, and salary hike might not be keeping up with expectations.

Work Conditions.
Overtime: 71.7% of employees work overtime.
Travel Frequency: 70.95% don’t travel, 18.84% travel frequently, 10.2% travel rarely.
Overtime burden is very high, which could be contributing to burnout. Majority are not traveling for work, which could affect exposure and growth opportunities in some roles.

Age Distribution.
The 30s age group is the largest, with 533 employees (314 females, 219 males).
20s and 40s follow in volume. Very few employees are aged 50+ or under 20.
The workforce is mid-career dominated, indicating potential upcoming demands for career growth or leadership roles.

Distance from Home. 
A significant concentration of employees live <10 km from work. Females are more likely to live closer to the office than males.
Long commute might be a male-specific stressor, and this factor could influence attrition or job satisfaction.

Education Level. 
Majority of employees hold a Bachelor’s degree (473, ~38%), Followed by Master’s (27.6%) and College (19.3%), Very few have Doctorates or below-college education
The workforce is well-educated, likely expecting professional growth and skill development opportunities.

Education Field by Gender.
Life Sciences and Medical fields dominate: 
Life Sciences: 315 females, 202 males
Medical: 230 males, 171 females
Technical degrees and HR fields are significantly lower in volume.
The company is heavily invested in science-based roles, possibly R&D or healthcare.

Training Last Year. 
Most employees received 2 or 3 training sessions last year. Training is fairly balanced across genders. Training appears consistent, but further analysis is needed to see if those with fewer training sessions have higher attrition.

Department & Job Role by Gender.
Sales Executives is the largest role (148 females, 157 males) — very balanced. Research Scientists is the second largest (148 males, 97 females) Lab Technicians and Healthcare Reps also form a big share.
Leadership roles (like Managers and Directors) have low representation, especially for females. Women are well represented in operational roles, but less so in leadership, especially in: Research Director, Manufacturing Director, Sales Manager.

Potential Attrition Indicators 
Gender imbalance in senior roles may limit retention for female high-performers.
Employees with longer commutes (especially men) might face burnout.
Training and career development need alignment with expectations — most employees are educated and mid-career.

Overall Attrition Rate: About 16% of employees have left the company — a non-negligible churn rate worth digging into.
Slightly more females left than males, but not drastically different.
Sales Representatives and Laboratory Technicians have the highest attrition rates. Research Directors and Manufacturing Directors have lower attrition. Sales has a higher attrition rate compared to R&D and HR.
Employees who work overtime have a much higher attrition rate. This could be a red flag on work-life balance or burnout. 
Single employees are more likely to leave compared to married or divorced employees.

Additional Insights About Attrition
Employees who left earned less on average than those who stayed. Salary might be a major driver of attrition.
Leavers were, on average, younger. Younger employees may have fewer commitments and be more willing to explore other opportunities or switch jobs frequently.
Leavers have fewer years of overall experience. Less experienced employees might not be as deeply invested in the company or may be exploring their options.
Leavers had spent fewer years at the company. Employees are most at risk of leaving within their first few years — onboarding and early engagement are key.
On average, they lived further from work. Commute length might be a subtle but real factor contributing to turnover.
Leavers were in lower job levels on average. Employees in junior roles may feel less valued or see fewer growth opportunities.
Less time in their current role or with their manager. Frequent changes in management or lack of strong leadership connections may contribute to attrition.
